 "links": {
  "about": "The one https-link rule every pairing's link check uses: the raw string holds no whitespace (Unicode White_Space), control character (Unicode Cc) or backslash; it parses as an absolute URL by the WHATWG URL Standard's basic URL parser with no base, whose host parser reads a domain by UTS #46 ToASCII over Unicode 17.0.0 data with the flags the Standard sets (CheckBidi, CheckJoiners, nontransitional processing); its scheme is https, compared case-insensitively (RFC 3986 §3.1); it has a host, a DNS name (RFC 1123 labels) or an IP literal (RFC 3986 §3.2.2), and no userinfo (RFC 3986 §3.2.1). Each row gives the link and whether the rule accepts it. 'refusedAs' is what a pairing's reading gives the same value as the legalContextUrl and as the agreement URL, alike, so that one value has one meaning everywhere: link-not-https only for a string of at most 2048 characters that parses as an absolute URL whose scheme is not https, and legal-context-malformed for every other value the rule refuses (not a string, empty, unparseable, no host, over 2048 characters, or an https URL the rule refuses). Card's, ack's and ap2's reading give legal-context-malformed for every value the rule refuses. 'placedAs' is what a pairing's placement (advertise, and MPP's and A2A's place) gives the same value as the link and as the agreement URL: link-not-https for every value the rule refuses, since the link MUST be https:// (LCP §8.1). A link's length is counted in UTF-16 code units, as JavaScript's String length counts it; LK24 to LK26 end in U+10000, one code point of two UTF-16 code units.",
